# Copy to .env (gitignored) and fill in the values.
#
# DATABASE_URL — Postgres connection string. `npm run db:up` starts a local
# Postgres 16 on port 55433 that matches this default:
DATABASE_URL=postgres://tooltrack:tooltrack@127.0.0.1:55433/tooltrack

# Port the Node API listens on. Cloud/container runtimes set this themselves;
# locally it defaults to 37411 when unset.
# PORT=37411

# Origins allowed to call this API from a browser carrying a session cookie,
# comma separated. Unset means localhost:5173 (and 127.0.0.1:5173) outside
# production, and nothing in production — credentialed CORS cannot use a wildcard, so a
# deployment that really splits the origins has to name the frontend here. In
# development Vite proxies /api instead, and this stays unset.
# TOOLTRACK_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://tooltrack.example.com

# Session cookies are marked Secure only when NODE_ENV=production, so that a
# plain http://localhost session still works in development. Never run a public
# deployment without it.
# NODE_ENV=production

# How long a login stays valid. Whole days, defaults to 30. Every session is an
# opaque token; only its SHA-256 hash is stored, so lowering this is the only
# way to shorten an already-issued login besides revoking it.
# SESSION_TTL_DAYS=30

# Bootstrap admin. A comma-separated allowlist of accounts that resolve as
# `admin` whatever account.user_account says. It exists so the first admin can
# sign in on a fresh database (every registration is a viewer) and so the last
# admin cannot lock everyone out. Prefer promoting a real account and then
# unsetting this. See README "First admin".
# TOOLTRACK_BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_EMAILS=

# Largest JSON request body accepted, in bytes. Defaults to 1 MiB. Tool cards
# are small; the cap is here so a stray upload is refused as 413 rather than
# buffered. It does not apply to the routes that take a file — photographs get
# TOOLTRACK_MAX_PHOTO_BYTES and a manual gets its own 40 MiB ceiling.
# MAX_REQUEST_BODY_BYTES=1048576

# Where photo files are written. Defaults to .local/media next to the code,
# which is gitignored. The database only stores what a photo is and which key
# holds it; the bytes live here. Point it at a mounted volume in a container —
# a container filesystem is not a place to keep the only copy of anything.
# TOOLTRACK_MEDIA_DIR=/var/lib/tooltrack/media

# How long a cutout preview stays available before it is swept, in whole
# minutes. Defaults to 30 — long enough to compare two methods and think about
# it, short enough that an afternoon of experimenting does not accumulate.
# Previews live in <media>/previews and nothing in the catalog points at them,
# so nothing is lost when one expires: it is generated again.
# TOOLTRACK_PREVIEW_TTL_MINUTES=30

# Largest photo accepted, on upload and when fetching a URL. Defaults to 10 MiB.
# TOOLTRACK_MAX_PHOTO_BYTES=10485760

# Image search keys. Since the settings screen exists these are a bootstrap,
# not the configuration: an admin sets keys at /settings, they are stored in
# the database, and a value saved there wins over anything here. These stay for
# handing a container a key it should not store, and for the first run.
#
# Which engines are used is decided per installation and per account on that
# screen, not by a variable.
#
# Google Custom Search — the best results for a specific model. Key from
# https://console.cloud.google.com (enable "Custom Search API" in the same
# project), search engine id from https://programmablesearchengine.google.com
# with image search on. Free tier 100 queries a day.
# GOOGLE_SEARCH_API_KEY=REPLACE_ME
# GOOGLE_SEARCH_CX=REPLACE_ME
#
# Brave Search — its own index, key from https://api-dashboard.search.brave.com
# with a free monthly allowance and a signup that takes a minute.
# BRAVE_SEARCH_API_KEY=REPLACE_ME
#
# Openverse needs no key at all and is on by default. It indexes openly
# licensed photography, so it finds "angle grinder" but not "GWS 180-LI".

# Cutout configuration is a bootstrap here, like the search keys: an admin sets
# the method and the keys at /settings, they are stored in the database, and a
# value saved there wins over anything below.
#
# "Wytnij samo narzędzie" — the cutout behind
# POST /api/tools/:id/photos/:photoId/cutout. Without a key the route answers
# 503 and the button says why; nothing else is affected.
#
# remove.bg is purpose-built for this and returns a transparent PNG. A key is
# free for 50 images a month at https://www.remove.bg/api.
# REMOVE_BG_API_KEY=REPLACE_ME
#
# Gemini is the general model: it returns the tool on a plain white background
# rather than a true cutout. Key from https://aistudio.google.com/apikey — a
# different key from GOOGLE_SEARCH_API_KEY, and a different product.
# GEMINI_API_KEY=REPLACE_ME
#
# `local` needs no key: no cost, an answer in milliseconds. It floods the
# background in from the edge of the frame and keeps the largest object — the
# method from the Qbrick labelling project — so it works on catalogue photos (a
# tool on a plain light background, which is what the web search imports) and
# refuses anything else with a 422 rather than guessing.
#
# Which methods may be used is decided at /settings and stored in the database.
# There is no environment variable for it: the switch belongs to an admin with
# a screen in front of them, not to a deploy.
#
# The one knob of the local method: sum of R+G+B at or above which a pixel
# counts as background. 735 is an average of 245 per channel. Lower it for
# greyish backgrounds, raise it for a very light tool.
# TOOLTRACK_CUTOUT_THRESHOLD=735

# Reported by GET /api/version so a running container can be identified. The
# Docker build passes it in; unset it reads 'unknown'.
# TOOLTRACK_COMMIT=unknown
